    <h1>Refractive Systems: Designing Spatial & Liquid Glass Interfaces</h1>
    <p>By Aarav Mehta & Elena Rostova | ISBN: 978-81-974021-1-4</p>
  </header>
  <main>
    <section><h2>Chapter 1: The Optics of Digital Translucency</h2><p>Every translucent surface in a digital interface makes a promise about spatial order. When a card refracts the canvas beneath it, the reader subconsciously infers elevation, materiality, and focus. Yet translucency without discipline quickly degrades into visual noise.

In ENOX's optical framework, we separate every glass plane into three measurable layers: the base backdrop attenuation (blur + saturation), the body tint luminance, and the 1-pixel specular rim that anchors the boundary against shifting backgrounds.</p></section>
<section><h2>Chapter 2: Typographic Contrast on Frosted Planes</h2><p>High-trust reading demands unwavering contrast. By pairing deep obsidian-navy ink (#0B1120) with a minimum 72% porcelain-white surface opacity, body copy maintains WCAG AAA readability even when chromatic artwork passes beneath the viewport.</p></section>
  </main>
